WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.200
Sometime in the next decade, the International
Space Station will begin a slow descent from

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.200
orbit.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:14.110
A gradual decline in its 400 kilometers altitude,
bringing the massive structure down to thicker

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:20.930
and thicker layers of our atmosphere before
it reaches a point of no return, 280 kilometers

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:22.810
above our heads.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:28.430
From here air resistance will rapidly slow
down the station and begin to tear it apart.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:34.129
Disintegrating over a planned debris field
centered around Point Nemo, the furthest point

00:00:34.129 --> 00:00:36.239
from any landmass on Earth.

00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:39.449
The South Pacific Oceanic Uninhabited Area.

00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:46.670
Here, in Earth's watery spacecraft graveyard,
the remains of the ISS will lay after over

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.170
2 decades of valuable service to humankind.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.469
But this all begs the question.

00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:52.469
Why?

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:55.679
Why decommission such a valuable piece of
space infrastructure?

00:00:55.679 --> 00:01:00.839
The ISS has been growing gradually since the
very first components of the station reached

00:01:00.839 --> 00:01:01.839
orbit.

00:01:01.839 --> 00:01:08.660
On November 20th 1998, the Zarya Cargo Module,
the first module of the ISS, launched aboard

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:12.090
a Russian Proton Rocket from Kazakhstan. [1]

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:15.340
Zarya was the seed for what was to come.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:21.450
Carrying with it the docking, electrical power,
propulsion and guidance systems needed to

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:28.260
gradually grow what was to become an unprecedented
piece of international space infrastructure.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.960
Zarya was joined just two weeks later by the
Unity Module.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:38.030
A module with 2 axial connection points, one
on either end of the module, and 4 radial

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:41.030
connection points around its circumference.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:46.670
Where the first truss segments would be attached,
allowing massive solar arrays to deploy and

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:50.000
provide power for the growing space laboratory.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.960
These sections of the International Station
remain in space to this day, providing for

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:04.500
the needs of over 250 individual astronauts
in orbit around Earth over the past 23 years.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:10.729
But this home in space was never intended
to be permanent, and many components are showing

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:11.909
their age.

00:02:11.909 --> 00:02:17.910
The Zarya module, that seed from which the
entire space station grew, has developed several

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:21.099
cracks that are going to continually grow.

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:27.569
Cyclical thermal expansion, caused by the
16 sunsets and sunrises the ISS witnesses

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:33.090
each and every day, stresses the structure
of the station and causes these modules to

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.709
gradually degrade over time.

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:40.489
While jolts from spacecraft docking with the
space station can also cause stress in the

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:41.489
structure.

00:02:41.489 --> 00:02:46.560
Not to mention the constant bombardment from
micrometeorites and space debris that has

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.400
damaged vital components of the ISS, like
the strike on the Canadarm in 2021.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.819
[2]
Mystery pressure leaks have even forced Astronauts

00:02:55.819 --> 00:03:01.280
into wild goose chases seaking the source
as their atmosphere slowly vents to space

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.609
[3] Problems like this have been cropping
up more and more frequently as the station

00:03:06.609 --> 00:03:07.920
ages.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.269
These modules, part of the primary structure
of the ISS, are what will limit the life of

00:03:13.269 --> 00:03:21.599
the station, and at some point the modules
will need to be retired, the question is when.

00:03:21.599 --> 00:03:28.030
Each participating partner of the ISS is expected
to inspect and perform life extending maintenance,

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:29.030
and while the

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:34.590
North American, Canadian, European and Japanese
space agencies have performed these checks

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:41.239
through to 2028, however, critically, Russia,
who have now become an international pariah,

00:03:41.239 --> 00:03:44.459
have only completed these checks through to
2024.

00:03:44.459 --> 00:03:50.730
[4] Limiting the ISS’s current lifetime
to just 2 years.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:56.239
The war in the Ukraine has thrown NASA’S
recent transition and decommissioning report,

00:03:56.239 --> 00:04:01.140
which planned to deorbit the ISS by 2031,
into limbo.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:06.870
Without Russian cooperation the transition
plan is unfeasible, as the International Space

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:13.249
Station was never designed to have large sections
of its structure removed to allow a participating

00:04:13.249 --> 00:04:16.019
partner to cease cooperation.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:21.380
Without Russia, we may only have 2 years of
operation of the International Space Station

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:27.440
left, potentially dooming NASAs future plans
laid out in the transition report.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.610
To understand what we may have just lost let’s
dive into the decommissioning and transition

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:33.610
report.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:34.610
Short pause in edit.

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:35.610
Flipping through pages of the report animation

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:39.229
NASA has been planning this decommissioning
for some time, and for good reason.

00:04:39.229 --> 00:04:44.419
The International Space Station costs NASA
roughly 3 billion dollar every year.

00:04:44.419 --> 00:04:49.340
With the costs split fairly evenly between
the cost of transporting astronauts and supplies

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:54.650
to orbit and system operations and maintenance
of the station itself.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:05:01.111
To put that into perspective, 3 billion is
over 10% of NASA’s total 24 billion dollar

00:05:01.111 --> 00:05:02.139
budget and over a third of their human space
flight budget.

00:05:02.139 --> 00:05:08.460
To really put that into perspective, the Perseverance
rover mission cost a total of 2.7 billion

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:09.460
dollars.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:10.460
[5]

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:13.690
These costs are the real reason the ISS will
be decommissioned.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:20.099
The transition report lays out a plan to decommission
the International Space Station to create

00:05:20.099 --> 00:05:26.590
up to 1.8 billion dollars a year in budget
that could be used for NASA’s moon colony

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:27.590
goals.

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:33.030
That’s exciting in itself, but is losing
the international space station in the process

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:34.030
worth it?

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:40.349
Well, luckily, the transition plan lays out
how NASA will not only free up 1.8 billion

00:05:40.349 --> 00:05:45.690
dollars of annual budget, but also maintain
a presence in orbit.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:50.170
Commercial space flight has rapidly changed
the face of the space industry in the last

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:51.170
10 years.

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:56.630
In fact with the current state of affairs
with the war in Ukraine, without SpaceX, NASA

00:05:56.630 --> 00:06:02.580
would be unable to send astronauts to the
ISS, as the Falcon 9 is the only American

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:07.319
rocket fully certified for transporting humans
to the ISS.

00:06:07.319 --> 00:06:12.550
It is this advancement in the private space
industry that is going to allow NASA to maintain

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:19.690
a presence in orbit, while saving enough money
to fund a perseverance rover every 18 months.

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:24.949
The bulk of this money saving will be achieved
by offloading the development and maintenance

00:06:24.949 --> 00:06:30.449
of the next generation of orbiting space habitats
to the commercial space industry.

00:06:30.449 --> 00:06:36.659
NASA has long desired to do this, and the
benefits are clear with the success of SpaceX.

00:06:36.659 --> 00:06:41.970
Whose innovation and pricing have forced the
entire industry to adapt.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:46.419
Commercial industry is competitive, but in
order to allow the commercial space industry

00:06:46.419 --> 00:06:50.669
to flourish in low earth orbit, there must
be something to sell.

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:53.780
A viable economy must exist.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:59.150
In order to stimulate this new economy, NASA
has conducted studies to identify the most

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:04.800
promising enterprises that could find their
home in the next generation space station,

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.699
and several of those enterprises were part
of the transition plan for the ISS.

00:07:10.699 --> 00:07:15.050
NASA identified a couple of key industries
that could flourish in space.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:18.490
The first of which is the entertainment industry.

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:22.069
Imagine a world where the battle room of Ender’s
game is possible.

00:07:22.069 --> 00:07:27.090
That’s a world that Space Entertainment
Enterprises is seeking to create.

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:32.479
With their spherical inflatable movie studio,
they are slated to film an unnamed movie with

00:07:32.479 --> 00:07:35.460
Tom Cruise as the leading actor.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:38.800
This however would not be a one off production.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.810
The studio would be rentable by anyone with
a high enough budget.

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:42.810
A brave new world where I would put money
on MrBeast flying himself to space for a video.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:43.810
Isn’t that right Jimmy?

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:45.750
This isn’t just a pie in the sky idea.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:53.199
In 2020, NASA awarded Axiom space a contract
worth 140 million dollars to launch a module

00:07:53.199 --> 00:08:00.210
that would attach to the ISS in 2024, which
will provide accommodation for private astronauts

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.879
and continue scientific research.

00:08:02.879 --> 00:08:07.569
This is the first step in a planned expansion
of a private section of the International

00:08:07.569 --> 00:08:13.159
Station, which, if successful, will be followed
by additional modules that would allow the

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:19.659
Axiom space station to operate completely
independently from the ISS and expand on the

00:08:19.659 --> 00:08:25.190
other industries that NASA identified as potentially
profitable space economies.

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:30.610
The Space Entertainment Enterprises module
is part of this plan.

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:35.029
Microgravity manufacturing was another industry
identified.

00:08:35.029 --> 00:08:39.099
Manufacturing without the influence of gravity
has some major advantages that are just waiting

00:08:39.099 --> 00:08:44.880
for the right product that can justify the
cost of shipping raw materials to space.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.560
Processes that are driven by gravity, like
sedimentation, convection, and hydrostatic

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:53.970
pressure are eliminated, which affect manufacturing
processes.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:59.680
Sedimentation for example makes it very difficult
to create an evenly mixed liquid, as denser

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:01.670
particles sink to the bottom.

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:06.130
Say when we are creating an alloyed metal
with difficult density elements.

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:11.410
[6] These non-uniformities can affect the
material properties of the final metal component,

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:18.550
and so there is huge potential to create new
higher quality superalloys in space.

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:24.150
This is just one of many potential manufacturing
techniques that could be employed in space,

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:28.089
and we will discuss the topic in detail in
a future video.

00:09:28.089 --> 00:09:33.380
With the current transition plan, this Axiom
commercial space station will detach from

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:40.230
the ISS in 2028, becoming the new seed from
which the space station can continue to grow

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:45.920
and provide a space for future NASA astronauts
to work from, with rental payments due to

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.100
Axiom and transportation costs to SpaceX.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:55.450
There is a lot to look forward to with that
plan, but what are the chances this goes ahead

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:59.920
with Russia’s invasion of Ukraine making
it impossible to work with them.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:05.870
Russia’s segments rely on America’s segments
for power, and the USA segments rely on Russian

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:10.089
propulsion to stay in orbit and avoid space
debris.

00:10:10.089 --> 00:10:15.670
This is an international space station that
relies on international cooperation.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:21.070
It’s clear that Russian astronauts oppose
the war in Ukraine, based on their recent

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:25.010
uniform change, but they do not call the shots
here.

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:30.490
The reality is, if this continues, NASA will
need to make some major decisions in the next

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:31.490
two years.

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:34.440
There are a few potential outcomes here.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:37.339
The Russian’s separating their modules is
not one of them.

00:10:37.339 --> 00:10:42.269
[7] As they need that power from the rest
of the ISS and would struggle to complete

00:10:42.269 --> 00:10:45.430
that work without the help of the Canadian
robotic arm.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:50.639
Russia could, under some miracle, get its
shit together in the next 2 years and remove

00:10:50.639 --> 00:10:55.089
Putin’s regime and continue international
cooperation in space.

00:10:55.089 --> 00:11:01.270
Or, Russia's segments may not be certified
through to 2028, forcing the deorbiting of

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:05.970
the International Space Station to be advanced,
with the transition to a commercial space

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:07.750
station abandoned.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:14.500
Alternatively, emergency funds could be allocated
to NASA to rapidly supply the ISS with additional

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:19.899
modules to replace Russian services, like
propulsion for orbit maintenance and debris

00:11:19.899 --> 00:11:24.430
avoidance, allowing the transition plans to
continue on as normal.

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:29.940
Or, the funds could simply be allocated to
the commercial space companies to accelerate

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:36.949
transition plans, but that is a massive ask
for unproven vendors in a 2 year timeframe.

00:11:36.949 --> 00:11:38.340
One thing is for sure.

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:42.010
The current structure of the ISS is on borrowed
time.

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:47.201
Its aging primary structure is becoming a
larger and larger issue every year, and at

00:11:47.201 --> 00:11:51.240
some point in the next decade, it will be
deorbited.

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.730
The only question now is when.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:58.459
It’s hard to believe that the International
Space Station has been in orbit for just over

00:11:58.459 --> 00:11:59.589
twenty years.

00:11:59.589 --> 00:12:04.690
It’s been a huge source of inspiration for
aspiring engineers and scientists around the

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:08.060
world, a frontier of scientific research.

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:13.670
With only 253 individual astronauts from 19
different countries getting the privilege

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:20.209
to visit and work from the ISS, it’s seen
by many as the pinnacle scientific career

00:12:20.209 --> 00:12:21.209
achievement.

00:12:21.209 --> 00:12:25.149
A joy only a few of us will ever get to experience.

